UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 8, Lesson 1: Dreaming stories and their meaning', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Dreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and responsibilities and are not just fairytales. Some are shared publicly by Custodians for education and others are not for sharing, so students use only stories that Custodians or First Nations authors have published, and they do not invent or guess.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich is the better source: a website with no author, or a book credited to named Custodians of a Nation? Give a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRead or listen to one published story by a First Nations storyteller. Complete a source card with title, storyteller, Nation, publisher and one thing the story teaches the reader about responsibility. Do not retell the story from memory.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Dreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and responsibilities and are not just fairytales. Some are shared publicly by Custodians for education and others are not for sharing, so students use only stories that Custodians or First Nations authors have published, and they do not invent or guess.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-ACH-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Read or listen to one published story by a First Nations storyteller. Complete a source card with title, storyteller, Nation, publisher and one thing the story teaches the reader about responsibility. Do not retell the s Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy. Reasoning: I used story by attending to Custodians and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians. Correction: The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy. Why: the correction restores accurate use of story.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating story on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with story, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy. This matches the lesson skill for story.","options":["Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy."],"question":"Which is the better source: a website with no author, or a book credited to named Custodians of a Nation? Give a reason. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Dreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and responsibilities and are not just fairytales. Some…","The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy.","Careful use of Custodians and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising story?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named Custodians and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the Custodians and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising story. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about story?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about story.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about Custodians and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: a class library book is credited to a named First Nations storyteller, names the Nation and credits the artist. The teacher reads…","Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Dreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians.","Read or listen to one published story by a First Nations storyteller. Complete a source card with title, storyteller, Nation, publisher and one…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of story?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating story on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy. Reasoning: I used story by attending to Custodians and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with story, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians. Correction: The book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy. Why: the correction restores accurate use of story.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w08-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise story.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"First Nations Histories and Cultures","success_criteria":["I can use story with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify Custodians and explain why it matters.","I can practise permission independently on a new prompt.","I can notice source needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Dreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and responsibilities and are not just fairytales. Some are shared publicly by Custodians for education and others are not for sharing, so students use only stories that…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Dreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and responsibilities and are not just fairytales. Some are shared publicly by Custodians for education and others are not for sharing, so students use only stories that Custodians or First Nations authors have published, and they do not invent or guess.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class library book is credited to a named First Nations storyteller, names the Nation and credits the artist. The teacher reads the book as it is written instead of retelling it from memory. The class records the title, storyteller, Nation and publisher on a source card.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhich is the better source: a website with no author, or a book credited to named Custodians of a Nation? Give a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy.","explain":"Dreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and responsibilities and are not just fairytales. Some are shared publicly by Custodians for education and others are not for sharing, so students use only stories that Custodians or First Nations authors have published, and they do not invent or guess.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Stories carry law and knowledge.","caption":"Some stories are not for sharing.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Story</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">holds law and knowledge</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Fairytale?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">a mistaken label</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: a class library book is credited to a named First Nations storyteller, names the Nation and credits the artist. Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: a class library book is credited to a named First Nations storyteller, names the Nation and credits the artist. Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class library book is credited to a named First Nations storyteller, names the Nation and credits the artist. The teacher reads the book as it is written instead of retelling it from memory. The class records the title, storyteller, Nation and publisher on a source card. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"A source card for a published story.","caption":"Credit the storyteller and Nation.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Source card</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">title, storyteller, Nation</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Permission</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">shared by Custodians</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians. Here is a more productive model: Example: a class library book is credited to a named First Nations storyteller, names the Nation and credits the artist. The teacher reads the book as it is written instead of retelling it from memory. The class records the title, storyteller, Nation and publisher on a source card.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe book credited to Custodians, because it shows who holds the story and that they chose to share it. The unnamed website cannot show permission or accuracy.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking Dreaming stories are made-up fairytales, or that any version is as good as another, ignores that they carry law and knowledge held by Custodians. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDreaming stories belong to particular Nations and families. They carry law, history, science, geography and responsibilities and are not just fairytales. Some are shared publicly by Custodians for education and others are not for sharing, so students use only stories that Custodians or First Nations authors have published, and they do not invent or guess. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 8, Lesson 1: Dreaming stories and their meaning","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"First Nations Peoples and Country","week":8,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a class library book is credited to a named First Nations storyteller, names the Nation and credits the artist. The teacher reads the book as it is written instead of retelling it from memory. The class records the title, storyteller, Nation and publisher on a source card.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Stories carry law and knowledge.","caption":"Some stories are not for sharing.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Story</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">holds law and knowledge</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Fairytale?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">a mistaken label</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A source card for a published story.","caption":"Credit the storyteller and Nation.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Source card</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">title, storyteller, Nation</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Permission</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">shared by Custodians</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y3-w08-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
